# demo.script - boot to the main menu, start the rolling demo, and let it run
# for a fixed span so both builds can be compared over the same content.
#
# Run with `make recomp-demo-sound`, which sets RECOMP_AUDIO_TRACE and writes the
# trace where compare_sounds.py expects it.
#
# WHY THE ROLLING DEMO AND NOT A HAND-PLAYED SESSION
#
# The sound comparison needs both sides to cover the same content. A person
# playing the original and a script playing ours are not the same content, and
# every difference in the counts then has two possible causes. The rolling demo
# is the same sequence every time on both sides, and it is the game playing
# itself, so it exercises the effect path rather than only the menu.
#
# WHERE THE MENU ENTRY IS
#
# 640x480, the nine entries centred on x=320 at roughly:
#
#     NEW GAME 154   TUTORIAL 182   LOAD GAME 211   MULTIPLAYER 240
#     OPTIONS 269    ROLLING DEMO 298   CREDITS 327   COMING SOON 356
#     QUIT 385
#
# Read off build/recomp/smoke/frames/smoke_menu_present.ppm rather than guessed.
# ROLLING DEMO is the sixth, at y=298.
#
# Note the menu also has COMING SOON, which is the other video entry. The
# handler at 004c3540 plays FMV\trailer.tgq and is reached from the entry
# record at 005d7d74; do not assume that record is this one without checking,
# because a run that lands on a trailer video plays no effects at all and would
# look like a build with no sound rather than the wrong menu entry. The dumps
# below are what tells the two apart.
#
# HOW LONG
#
# 180 seconds from launch, which is the span the original side is stopped at,
# and it has to be exactly that: the two sides are only comparable over the
# same span. The waits below sum to 180000 ms - 22300 to get from launch to the
# demo running, then 150000 of demo in five 30-second steps, then 7700 to the
# end. An earlier version summed to 201300 and the host exited at about 197,
# losing the final dump and both expectations, which is how a run that had
# stopped early looked identical to one that had finished.

wait 16000
dump demo_menu

# Home the cursor into the corner first: buffered DirectInput events are
# deltas, so an absolute destination on its own moves nothing.
moveby -2000 -2000
wait 400
moveby 320 298
wait 900
dump demo_hover
click left 320 298
wait 5000
dump demo_started

# The demo itself. Dumped every 30 s so a run that fell back to the menu, or
# sat on a black video frame, is visible in the frames rather than only in the
# counts.
wait 30000
dump demo_30s
wait 30000
dump demo_60s
wait 30000
dump demo_90s
wait 30000
dump demo_120s
wait 30000
dump demo_150s
wait 7700
dump demo_end

# Enough to tell a run that ran from one that never started. Deliberately not
# the level1.script expectations: the demo may never enter the 3D scene if the
# entry turns out to be a video, and this fixture is for the sound counts.
expect presents>50
expect present_changes>200

quit
